  mut .findFirst(f: read F[E, Bool]): mut Opt[E] -> this.filter f .first;
  mut .findMap[R:*](f: read F[E, mut Opt[R]]): mut Opt[R] -> this.mapFilter f .first;
  mut .any(f: read F[E, Bool]): Bool -> this.filter f .first .isSome;
  mut .none(f: read F[E, Bool]): Bool -> this.any f .not;
  mut .all(f: read F[E, Bool]): Bool -> this.none{e -> f#e .not};
  }
```

`down.push#(down, e)` sends `e` downstream; `down.pushOpt#(down, o)` sends the element of `o` downstream when `o` is not empty.
`Sort#(xs, by, k)` and `Max#(xs, by)` are the merge tree and the max tree of **The calls of `by`**. `Append#(xs, b)` is the fresh list of the elements of `xs` followed by those of `b.seqFlow`, each as `read/imm E`.

### Sources and modes

The mode decides which strategies the terminal may pick:
- `Seq`: only the sequential strategy, over any elements.
- `ParRead`: the parallel strategies too, over `read` or `imm` elements.
- `ParImm`: as `ParRead`, and the source elements are `imm`, as the condition of `.forEach` and the speculative strategy of `.for` need (see **for and forEach**).
- `ParRepr` over `mut Repr[T]` and `ParPod` over `mut IsoPod[T]`: the parallel strategies too, over elements each owning its mutable state.

The source records the mode:
- `.flow` with no argument exists only where the type of the receiver shows that every element is `imm`, and records `ParImm`: `.flow` on an imm `List`, `EList`, `Map` or `Opt`, on a `Set` and an `ESet`, `.keys` of a `Map`, `UStr`, and the ranges (`0 =~~= 199`, `.flow(step)`).
- `.seqFlow`, on every `ToFlow`, records `Seq`.
- `xs.flow(mode)` on a read or mut `List`, `EList`, `Map` or `Opt` records the mode passed; the type of `mode` checks the elements: `ParImm[T:imm]`, `ParRead[T:imm,read]`, `ParRepr[T]` over `mut Repr[T]`, `ParPod[T]` over `mut IsoPod[T]`. The flow object keeps the mode passed, and the checks tell the modes apart by its type.
- `Flows#` records `Seq`.

A mode is what the chain records, not a Fearless type: `ParImm`, `ParRead`, `ParRepr` and `ParPod` are also the types of the argument of `.flow(mode)`, while `Seq` has no type and is recorded by `.seqFlow` and `Flows#`.

`ToFlow` is the finished collections: `List`, `EList`, `Set`, `ESet`, `Map`, `Opt`, the ranges and `UStr`. `.seqFlow` on a `ToFlow` runs no user code: it always terminates, never throws, and gives the same elements in the same order for structurally equivalent receivers. `ToFlow` is sealed so that no user type can break this, and `Flow` is not a `ToFlow`. The lambdas of `flatMap` and `flatMapMut` return a `ToFlow`, and `++` takes one, so a `Set` or an `Opt` needs no `.list` first; the runtime can iterate a `ToFlow` it receives directly, with no flow object and no copy. `Map` is a `ToFlow[mut KeyElem[K,E]]`: through a `read` map the entries are `read KeyElem`s, whose `.elem` is `read/imm E`; a `KeyElem[K,E]` written with no capability is `imm`, so as a `ToFlow[KeyElem[K,E]]`, or with `Flow[KeyElem[K,read/imm E]]` from `read .flow(mode)`, a `read` map would give `imm` entries whose `imm .elem` is an `imm` view of an element others may mutate; the `read` and `mut` variants of `.flow(mode)` give `read KeyElem` and `mut KeyElem` for the same reason.

Whether an element is `imm` or only `read` is known to the type system alone: the runtime can not tell them apart. So `ParImm` is never discovered from the elements: it comes from `.flow` with no argument, where the type of the receiver shows it, or from `ParImm#`, where the type of `mode` checks it.

Every strategy, the sequential strategy included, runs the mode checks before any element is sent downstream: the identity check over a source of mode `ParRepr` or `ParPod`, the onlyImmCapture check on every lambda of a flow of mode `ParRepr` or `ParPod` except those of `for`, `forEach` and `let`, the check that no stage/terminal of a flow of mode `ParRepr` or `ParPod` needs positive speculation calls, the condition of `.forEach`, and the deceptive shape exceptions. Each throws a misuse exception.

### Core methods

The core methods have a direct implementation. Each is core because no combination of the other methods can express it:

- **The sources** are `_Magic!`: they are where the elements come from.
- **`fold`** builds the accumulator with `acc#` and calls `f` on it and each element in flow order: every call observes what the calls before it left in the accumulator. It is core because it is the one terminal that trickles: `allToAll` followed by a loop over the list would be a fence.
- **`for(a, f)`** calls its lambda on every element, in flow order, once every stage has finished with every element, passing `a` to every call, as `Block.loop(a, body)` does. It is core because its lambda is `mut`, and `fold` and `allToAll` take only `read` lambdas. `for(f)` is this one with a value its lambda ignores.
- **`forEach(a, f)`** is `for(a, f)` without the fence: it calls its lambda on each element, in flow order, as soon as the stages have finished with that element; it trickles, as `fold` does. It is core for the same reason as `for`, and it throws a misuse exception unless the condition of `.forEach` holds (see **Misuse exceptions**). `forEach(f)` is this one with a value its lambda ignores.
- **`actorMut`** runs its lambda on every element in flow order, passing it the sink, the current state and the element; the lambda sends zero or more elements downstream through the sink and returns `Continue#` or `Break#`. It is the only stage that keeps state across its calls and the only stage that can stop receiving elements: every other stage with one of those two abilities desugars to it. The lambda returns `Continue#` or `Break#`, the same values `Block.loop` uses; `OuterReturn#v`, which in a loop skips the rest of the enclosing block and makes `v` its result, does not typecheck as the result of an `actor` lambda. Its state and its sink are `mutH`: see **actor and actorMut**.
- **`actor`** is `actorMut` with a `mut` sink, the state passed as it is, and only `imm` elements sent downstream. The same code implements both.
- **`allToAll`** collects every element it receives into a list, passes the list to `f` once, and sends downstream the elements of the list `f` returns, in order. It is `.list |> f` followed by flowing the result, but lazy and inside the chain: nothing runs until the terminal is called, the mode and the checks are those of the whole flow expression, and the stages after it are stages of the same chain. It is core because no `actorMut` can keep the elements it has seen: the hygienic state takes only `iso` and `imm` values, and a stage must not keep what it sends downstream (see **A stage sends an element downstream or keeps it, never both**). The list is fresh and owned by the flow, so `f` receives it `mut`; the elements it returns may be those it received, in any order and number, or new ones.
- **`distinct`** keeps the key `by#e .close` of each kept element, a `read K`, and sends each new element downstream at once, as `read` or `imm`. It is core because no actor can do both. `actorMut` can not keep the key: its hygienic state refuses a `read` key. `actor` can keep it, since its state is passed as it is, but it sends downstream only `imm` elements, and `distinct` sends the element itself, as `read` when it is `read` or `mut`. `actor` can not send `read` elements: its lambda could send its own state as `read`, and the stage after would read it while the next call mutates it. `distinct` never sends its state, which the signature of `actor` can not express.
- **`suppressDeceptiveShapesException`** records a flag in the chain and sends downstream every element it receives, unchanged; it has no desugaring since the flag is part of the chain.

### What the signatures do not show

**Fences and distinct.**\
`sort` is stable: elements that `by` ranks together keep their flow order. `max` and `min` send downstream every element tied for the maximum (minimum), in flow order: `Lists#(3,1,3).flow.max{::}.list` is `Lists#(3,3)`. `sortDistinct` keeps, for each class of elements `by` ranks together, the element with the smallest index, in `by` order: `Lists#(3,1,3,2,1).flow.sortDistinct{::}.list` is `Lists#(1,2,3)`. `sort`, `sortDistinct`, `max` and `min` take an `OrderBy`, not an `OrderHashBy`, so they never call `.hash`.
`distinct` keeps the same elements as `distinctMut`, sending each kept element downstream at once, as `read` or `imm`: `Lists#(3,1,3,2,1).flow.distinct{::}.list` is `Lists#(3,1,2)`, and so is `Lists#(3,1,3,2,1).flow.distinctMut{::}.list`.

**limit.**\
`.limit 0` throws a deterministic exception where `.limit` is called.

**sum and join.**\
The number types implement `SumNumber` as a fold with the checked `+`; the string types implement `JoinStr` as a fold.

### Lazy and single use

**Lazy:** No code executed as part of a stage runs until the terminal is called.

A flow is consumed by the call that adds the next stage or the terminal: calling a stage or a terminal on a flow that is already consumed throws. Nothing has run yet when this is detected, and whether a flow is consumed is known without looking at any element, so the exception is deterministic, thrown by that call, as for `.limit 0`.

### Flows trickle, except at fences

Observably, the flow must behave as if it trickled the elements through the stages one at a time.
It must not be observable whether a flow runs the stages one at a time or sends the elements one at a time through all the stages, whether it does internal materialisations, etc.

Fences are the only deviation: a fence sends nothing downstream until every stage before it has finished with every element.
An exception of a stage before the fence propagates before any exception caused by any stage after and included the fence.
A fence restricts what is observable, not when code runs: with `read` and `imm` elements the stages after a fence may run before it opens, as positive speculation calls, provided their exceptions are held until it opens.

Apart from `for` and `let`, the consuming terminals are not fences: they trickle like the stages. The work of the terminal on the element at flow order `j` comes before any stage runs on the element at flow order `j+1`, as far as it can be observed.
So a terminal with work of its own that can throw or not terminate is not `.list` followed by a loop. With `f` not terminating on `e1`, and `h` throwing on the first call,
`l.flow.map f .fold({0}, h)` throws: the sequential strategy calls `h` on `f#e0` and never processes `e1`. `.list` followed by a fold calls `f#e1` first, and never returns.
The same holds for `foldAcc`, `mapping` (a repeated key throws), `set` and `eSet` (the calls of `by`), `sum` (the checked `+` throws on overflow) and `join`.

### A `Break#` while the call before it is still running

A stage after an `actor` or `actorMut` may return `Break#` on an element the actor sent downstream while the actor call that sent it is still running: the elements of a `flatMapMut` expansion after the one that fills a `limit`, or the code between two calls of `down.push#`. That call is one the sequential strategy makes, and it completes: an element sent downstream after the break is dropped, the rest of the call runs, and what it throws or whether it terminates is part of what the whole flow expression returns; no element is processed once it has returned. With the actor ``{down, s, e -> Block#(down.push#(down, e), Error.msg`after`, Continue#)}`` before `.limit 1 .list`, the whole flow expression throws `after`. A code generator inlining the actor body lets the call finish and drops the elements sent downstream after the break; a `return` inside the loops of the pseudocode is legal where nothing the sequential strategy runs follows the `down.push#` in the same call, as in the finished list of a `flatMap` expansion and in the actor of `limit` itself. Under pipeline parallelism the actor node may already be running the calls on the elements after it when the break is received: those calls are positive speculation calls, cancelled as speculative calls are over `ParImm` and `ParRead`, with the usual holding, and not allowed over `ParRepr` and `ParPod`, so an actor whose stage after may break runs in lockstep with it.

### Every strategy returns what the sequential strategy returns

It must not be observable whether a potentially parallel flow actually runs in parallel or sequentially: every strategy must be observably equal to the sequential strategy.

Where a method contract is purposely under-specified, such as the tournament order in max, min and sort, the same choice must be made in every strategy: a different winner under a parallel strategy and the sequential strategy would make the strategy observable.

**Holding:**\
In every parallel strategy, an exception thrown by a call is held until two things are known: whether the sequential strategy makes that call at all, and whether every call the sequential strategy makes before it completes successfully. The exception propagates only if the sequential strategy makes that call and every call it makes before that call completes successfully. Otherwise the exception is discarded: for a positive speculation call when the sequential strategy never makes that call, for a negative speculation call when a call it makes before that call throws. If a call the sequential strategy makes before that call does not terminate, the exception is held forever. Speculative calls that can no longer affect the result can be cancelled only if they use `Yield#` correctly.

**Decision points:**\
A decision point is a call of an `actor` or `actorMut` lambda returning `Break#`: after it, the actor receives no other element. The call of the stage before it that sent the element downstream completes (see **A `Break#` while the call before it is still running**). `limit` desugars to such an `actorMut`, and the short-circuit terminals desugar to `.limit 1`, so they are the most common decision points, but not the only ones.
A call executed as part of a stage on an element is a positive speculation call when it runs before every decision point after that stage has decided whether that element is processed.

**Termination:**\
The whole flow expression returns as soon as the sequential strategy would return: no terminal waits for a call the sequential strategy does not make, or makes after a call that throws. A speculative call that does not terminate and never reaches a `Yield#` keeps its worker for the life of the process, but never delays the result. The output of a task is received by the terminal element by element, as the mailboxes of the pseudocode send it.

**Non deterministic exceptions of speculative calls:**\
Non deterministic exceptions are discarded exactly as the deterministic ones: a stack overflow or an out of memory error thrown by a call the sequential strategy does not make never propagates, and one thrown at a greater index than a deterministic exception never replaces it.
A worker may have a smaller stack than the calling worker, so a call may throw a stack overflow on one and complete successfully on the other: this is non determinism, allowed, but a cache of results (such as a memo) must never store a non deterministic exception, or every call through that cache from then on throws it.

### Cancellation: `Yield#`

`Yield: Sealed { #: Void }`. `Yield#` is a cancellation point: in a speculative call whose result can no longer affect what the whole flow expression returns, it stops that call, and nothing the call computed is used; in every other call, under every strategy, it returns `Void` and does nothing. So it is not observable, and it can sit in any loop or recursion that may run for long, in the lambdas of a flow or in the methods they call. A speculative call that does not terminate releases its worker only through `Yield#`.
`Yield#` can stop a call anywhere, inside `Repr.mutate` and `IsoPod.mutate` as elsewhere: a call whose result can no longer affect what the whole flow expression returns mutates only objects the flow created for elements whose results are discarded, or, when the flow throws, objects that are garbage collectable once the exception is caught, since every try has a `read` body; everything else it reads as `read` or `imm`. So nothing can observe what it leaves half done.

### The calls of `by`

**Under-specified, the same in every strategy:**\
A `by` is `imm`, so its calls are observable only through which one, among those that throw or do not terminate, is made before the others, and through their cost. Which calls are made is under-specified, and must stay so: the implementation needs the freedom to pick its algorithms. The only requirement is the same choice in every strategy. Promises beyond that should only exclude truly pointless freedom, and should be worded loosely. Candidates:
- `by.cmp(e, e)` is never made: comparing an element with itself is pointless.
- `.hash` is not called on the same element many times: the hash can be stored next to the element, as a hash set does anyway.
- `.cmp` is called only on elements whose hashes are equal, for the methods taking an `OrderHashBy`, where comparing elements with different hashes is pointless.
- `.cmp(a, b)` is called with `a` before `b` in flow order. This only removes the dependence on the order of the two arguments; different algorithms still compare different pairs, so a `by` that is not antisymmetric or not transitive still gives different results under different algorithms.
- The number of calls stays within the usual complexity of the method: about `n log n` for `sort`, about `n` for `max`, `min` and `distinct`.
The key `by#e` may be computed once per element and stored (decorate, sort, undecorate), which pays an expensive key, like a fitness, about `n` times instead of about `n log n` times; this changes the call sequence where the key is computed inside every comparison, and is allowed by the under-specification.

**Placement:**\
`distinct`, `distinctMut`, `mapping`, `set` and `eSet` place each key among the keys already present, calling `.hash` and `.cmp` of `by`: the placement. The placement can not be split, since it is where a repeated key is found; it makes its calls one at a time, in flow order, with one placement algorithm fixed once for every strategy.

**A `by` breaking its contract:**\
`by` is user code: nothing forces it to rank two structurally equivalent values together, or to respect the contract of an order (reflexive, antisymmetric, transitive). The contract is logical, not enforced, and no reasoning in this document may assume it.
`sort`, `sortDistinct`, `max`, `min`, `distinct`, `distinctMut`, `mapping`, `set` and `eSet` may throw a non deterministic exception when the answers of `by` show that it breaks its contract: `.cmp` not antisymmetric or not transitive on the pairs compared, or two elements ranked together with different hashes. They do not have to: detecting it is part of the chosen algorithm, as the calls are, so it is fixed once, and made the same way in every strategy: a flow expression that throws it under one strategy throws it under every strategy, and one that does not throw it under one does not throw it under any. An algorithm may detect only what its own calls show, with no extra call made just to check.
Where they do not throw, the result is whatever the chosen algorithm returns with those answers, the same in every strategy.

**One merge tree for every strategy:**\
Inserting each element as it is received suits pipeline parallelism, merging sorted chunks suits data parallelism, and sorting the whole list suits the sequential strategy; the three make different calls, so picking one per strategy would make the strategy observable whenever a call of `by` throws or does not terminate.

One choice serves every strategy: a stable merge sort over a tree fixed by the indices alone, the merge tree. Blocks `(0,1)`, `(2,3)`, `(4,5)`, `(6,7)` are merged, then `(0..1,2..3)` and `(4..5,6..7)`, then `(0..3,4..7)`; when the size is not a power of two, the blocks left over when the sort receives its last element are merged from the right. The tree depends on the size alone, not on chunk sizes or on the number of workers.
The merges have a fixed order: each runs as soon as both its blocks are finished, as in a binary counter, so when the sort receives the element at index `7` the merges `(6,7)`, `(4..5,6..7)` and `(0..3,4..7)` run in this order.
- Sequential strategy: the merges run in this order once every element has been received.
- Pipeline parallelism: the sort node places each element as it receives it, running each merge as a task of its own, so that a call of `by` that does not terminate does not stop the node from receiving an exception thrown by a stage before it.
- Data parallelism: a merge depends only on its two blocks, so a task can run any merge whose blocks are finished, on any worker. An exception thrown by a call of `by` is held until every stage before the sort and every call the sequential strategy makes before it have returned.
- Every merge compares an element of its left block with one of its right block, so in every `.cmp(a, b)` call `a` is before `b` in flow order.

The same tree serves `max` and `min` as the max tree: a merge compares the front elements of its two blocks and keeps the leading run of the winner, or both runs in order on a tie: one call per merge, seven over `l`. For `sortDistinct`, a merge drops the element of the right block of each pair ranked together.

`Sort#(vs, by, k)` calls `sort`, the merge tree under the sequential strategy, each merge keeping at most `k` elements:
```
List[Nat] sort(List[Nat] vs, OrderBy[Nat] by, Nat k){
  if(vs.isEmpty){ return vs; }
  EList[List[Nat]] blocks= EList#;
  for(Nat j: vs.indexes){
    List[Nat] b= Lists#(vs.get(j));
    for(Nat n= j; n % 2 == 1; n= n / 2){ b= merge(blocks.pop, b, by, k); }
    blocks.push(b);
    }
  while(blocks.size > 1){
    List[Nat] b= blocks.pop;
    blocks.push(merge(blocks.pop, b, by, k));
    }
  return blocks.pop;
  }
List[Nat] merge(List[Nat] a, List[Nat] b, OrderBy[Nat] by, Nat k){
  EList[Nat] res= EList#;
  Nat i= 0;
  Nat j= 0;
  while(res.size < k && i < a.size && j < b.size){
    if(by.cmp(a.get(i), b.get(j)).isGt){ res.add(b.get(j)); j= j + 1; continue; }
    res.add(a.get(i));
    i= i + 1;
    }
  while(res.size < k && i < a.size){ res.add(a.get(i)); i= i + 1; }
  while(res.size < k && j < b.size){ res.add(b.get(j)); j= j + 1; }
  return res.list;
  }
```
Adding the element at index `j` runs one merge per trailing `1` in the binary digits of `j`: the binary counter. The left block is popped after the right one, so `a` is before `b` in flow order.
`Max#(vs, by)` is the same tree with this merge in place of `merge`:
```
List[Nat] maxMerge(List[Nat] a, List[Nat] b, OrderBy[Nat] by){
  Ord o= by.cmp(a.get(0), b.get(0));
  if(o.isGt){ return a; }
  if(o.isLt){ return b; }
  return a ++ b;
  }
```
`MergeTree#(by, k)` makes the calls of `Sort#`, with each merge running as a task of its own as soon as its two blocks are complete. `s.add(x)` adds `x` as the element at the next index; it never waits for a call of `by` and never throws. `s.list` merges the blocks left over from the right, waits for the merges in the order `Sort#` makes them, and returns the result: if some calls of `by` throw or do not terminate, it returns what the one `Sort#` makes before the others returns. `MaxTree#by` is the same for `Max#`.

**The tree merged with the stages after it:**\
The tree is the chosen algorithm, and a stage after the sort may choose a cheaper tree, provided the choice is known before any element is processed, so that it is the same in every strategy.
With `.limit 3` after the sort, a merge stops once it has kept three elements: the rest of the merged block can not be among those three. Its calls are a prefix of those of the full merge, at most three per merge, with one merge fewer than the elements, and no block holds more than three elements. For a `by` respecting its contract the result is unchanged; for any other `by` the truncated tree is simply the chosen algorithm.
Under `.suppressDeceptiveShapesException`, `.sort by .first` may become a search for the minimum element with the smallest index; `.sort by .filter{::>3}.first` may sort only the elements passing the filter. Both change which calls of `by.cmp` are made, and both are allowed, like any other sorting algorithm. Sorting only the elements passing the filter also moves the calls of the filter, which is legal only when the filter can neither throw nor fail to terminate (see **sort then filter**).

### Misuse exceptions

A misuse exception is a non deterministic exception a flow expression throws because of how it is written: the identity check, the onlyImmCapture check, positive speculation calls over `ParRepr` and `ParPod`, and the deceptive shape exceptions. The misuse exceptions of a whole flow expression depend only on the flow expression and on its source, so its terminal throws them before any element is sent downstream. A misuse exception thrown by a flow nested inside a lambda is an exception of the call running that lambda: when that call is speculative, it is held and discarded as any other exception.
The mode checks are part of the semantics, not of the parallel strategies: every strategy runs them, the sequential strategy included, and a mode argument can never be accepted and ignored. The identity check reads the source and the onlyImmCapture check reads the lambdas; whether a flow expression needs positive speculation calls over `ParRepr` or `ParPod`, and so throws that misuse exception, is decided by its shape and its mode alone, as the deceptive shape exceptions are: never by the size of the source, the elements, the results of the lambdas, the number passed to `limit`, the number of workers or the size of the chunks.

**The identity check:**\
The flow runs an identity check over a source of mode `ParRepr` or `ParPod`, and throws a non deterministic exception when two elements are identity equal.
The identity check is not a consequence of racing: the sequential strategy runs it too, and skipping it would make the strategy observable. A parallel strategy may run it on the calling worker while the tasks run, provided it completes before the result of any task is used: when two tasks race on one `Repr`, the non deterministic exception is thrown whatever the race did.

**The onlyImmCapture check:**\
The backend marks each lambda object that captures only `imm` values; the flag is visible to the runtime (in Java), not in Fearless. A top level declaration used as a value captures nothing, so it carries the flag, and so does every `imm` lambda, which can capture only `imm` values.
No `imm` value can reach a `mut Repr` or `mut IsoPod`, so `ParRepr` and `ParPod` need the flag on every lambda of the flow except those of `for` and `let`, which run once every stage has finished, and of `forEach`, which has its own condition; without it the whole flow expression throws a misuse exception. With the flag, each task reaches no `Repr` or `IsoPod` but its own element.
When the lambdas are written in place, the flag is checked when compiling and leaves no code.

**Positive speculation calls over `ParRepr` and `ParPod`:**\
Over `ParRepr` and `ParPod` the `limit` of a flow expression, and the `limit 1` of a short-circuit terminal, allow parallelism only where they need no positive speculation calls.
A flow expression over `ParRepr` or `ParPod` with a stage/terminal that could run in parallel only with positive speculation calls on code that can reach an element of the source as `mut` throws a misuse exception, in every strategy, whatever fence comes before that stage/terminal: the mode asks for parallelism that the flow expression can not have. Ending the flow expression before that stage/terminal and running the rest with `.seqFlow` states the intent, and costs little: `.list` directly after a fence takes the list the fence has already materialised.

**Over `read` or `imm` elements:**\
A stage runs over `read` or `imm` elements when the shape and the mode show that no element can lead to a `mut` object existing before the flow: in mode `ParImm` or `ParRead`, or after a `++`, a `flatMap`, a `distinct`, an `actor` or a `scan`, which send their elements downstream as `read` or `imm`. The chain records no other element type, so a `map` returning an `imm` value shows nothing. A `mut` object a stage creates after that point is reachable from nowhere else.

**The condition of `.forEach`:**\
The segment of a `.forEach` is the stages between it and the last fence before it, or the source. `.forEach` throws a misuse exception when a stage of its segment can run in parallel, because the mode is not `Seq` or because a `++`, `flatMap`, `distinct`, `actor` or `scan` in the segment starts a data parallel region, unless all of these hold:
- the mode is `ParImm`;
- every lambda of the flow before the `.forEach` carries the onlyImmCapture flag;
- no `flatMapMut`, `actorMut`, `allToAll` with an arbitrary `f`, or `++` comes before it: the first three can send one `mut` object downstream as two elements, and `++` sends downstream the elements of its argument, which may reach `mut` objects the lambda of `.forEach` reaches too.

Then no stage can read what the lambda of `.forEach` writes: every `mut` object a stage reads was created by the flow for that element alone. With an empty segment, or in mode `Seq` with no stage of the segment starting a data parallel region, it never throws: only the sequential strategy runs the segment. Open decision: `ParRepr` and `ParPod` (item 12 of **Before Stage 1: decisions to take and things to fix**).

### Deceptive shape exceptions

A deceptive shape exception is the misuse exception of a sequence of stages/terminals in which a stage is pointless: in that shape, what the stage computes is discarded or overridden by the stages/terminals after it, and removing or moving the stage changes the result only when some call throws or does not terminate; or in which a stage is used in the variant whose extra, the `mut` elements it sends downstream, the stages/terminals after it discard; or which computes what a cheaper sequence computes, as a whole sort to read one element. The shape deceives the reader about what the flow expression does or costs. Such sequences are written by mistake, so the terminal throws a deceptive shape exception: a non deterministic exception naming the shape to write instead, as for the other misuse exceptions. The terminal sees the whole flow expression however it was built, so the check needs nothing from the compiler; a linter can report many of the same deceptive shape exceptions statically.
A deceptive shape exception is decided by the shape and the mode alone, together with the number type of the argument of `.sum`. It never depends on the values of the arguments: not on whether two `by` or two `p` are the same object or structurally equivalent, not on whether the list passed to `++` is empty, not on the number passed to `limit`. It never looks into the lambdas: not at what a lambda computes, nor at whether it can throw or not terminate. It never depends on the algorithm implementing a stage either: which calls of `.cmp` and `.hash` a stage makes stays under-specified. The optimiser is a separate matter: where the lambdas are simple enough, as `{::>3}` over a `Nat`, which can not throw, the backend may perform a rewrite that is not legal in general, but whether a sequence throws a deceptive shape exception never depends on it: `.sort by .filter{::>3}.list` throws a deceptive shape exception at every stage of the plan.
